<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#old-high-german">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old High German</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old High German is the earliest recorded stage of the German language, used from about the eighth to the eleventh century. It was spoken in the eastern Frankish realm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a> of 842 CE contain one of its earliest surviving texts, sworn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>'s troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-high-german.jpg" alt="Old High German" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hildebrandslied_Teil_1_von_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) divided the empire into kingdoms that foreshadowed France and Germany; the Oaths of Strasbourg (842 CE), sworn in both a Romance vernacular and <b>Old High German</b>, are the earliest surviving text in a language recognisable as early Old French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The standard theory of the Germanic languages places it in the East Germanic branch, beside a few very scantily attested languages, apart from North Germanic (Old Norse and its descendants) and West Germanic (Old English, <b>Old High German</b> and the rest).</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory was celebrated in the Ludwigslied, an <b>Old High German</b> poem of 59 rhyming couplets.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Wissembourg grew around the Benedictine abbey of Saints Peter and Paul, founded in the 7th century on the Lauter, where the monk Otfrid wrote his <b>Old High German</b> Gospel Harmony in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wissembourg">Free Imperial City of Wissembourg</a></li><li><blockquote>The cathedral library preserves the Merseburg Charms, the only surviving <b>Old High German</b> pagan spells.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-merseburg">Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg</a></li></ul></section>
